{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790268825,"nonce":"N+oCgB6TUdgeo0aPRARjzu68wk4=","title":"Python: a counted loop over range() with a zero step never raises ValueError","message":"`for q in range(a, b, s)` lowered as a counted loop does not check the step. With a literal zero step the loop never ends; with a step that is zero at run time the loop runs zero times and no ValueError is raised. The list path (zb_list_range, 5f17163) raises correctly.\n\n    def f():\n        try:\n            for q in range(1, 2, 0):\n                pass\n        except ValueError:\n            print(\"ValueError\")\n    f()    # CPython: ValueError; zypy main dea6d08b and later: hangs\n\n    def g(s):\n        try:\n            for q in range(1, 2, s):\n                pass\n        except ValueError:\n            print(\"ValueError\")\n    g(0)   # CPython: ValueError; zypy: prints nothing\n\nSince 9c19776d the builtin-alias rewrite sends `for q in xrange(1, 2, 0)` (with `xrange = range`) down the same counted path, so the aliased form, which raised through the list on main, now hangs too. Fix: the counted-loop lowering raises ValueError when the step is zero (a literal zero at compile time, a run-time check before the loop otherwise).","files":null}]}